I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, and the frozen reference/tooling layout. I’ll identify and checkpoint a complete multiphase model early, then refine only physically supported branches and export the requested GSAS-II project and artifacts.

The specimen is a CuO–WO₃ reaction fired at 700 °C, on a 10–100° Cu Kα₁/Kα₂ grid with unknown zero, profile, displacement, texture, and background. I’m querying only the frozen COD index for Cu–W–O products and exact precursor/intermediate compositions, while independently extracting the observed peak families.

Peak-family mapping already supports a chemically closed three-phase inventory: triclinic CuWO₄, cubic Cu₃WO₆, and monoclinic WO₃. A common ≈−0.45° displacement maps their strongest independent reflections (for example 29.55→29.09°, 15.65→15.18°, and the WO₃ triplet 23.15/23.61/24.37→22.67/23.08/23.89°). I’m now comparing the same-composition symmetry alternatives before committing the replayable GSAS-II checkpoint.

The first full checkpoint refines to Rwp ≈9.48% with preliminary crystalline fractions 65.9% CuWO₄, 14.9% Cu₃WO₆, and 19.2% WO₃. I’m now applying the identical staged refinement to monoclinic-setting, triclinic, orthorhombic, tetragonal, and high-pressure CuWO₄ alternatives; this keeps topology comparisons fair rather than selecting on stick-pattern appearance alone.

Topology coverage is decisive: the room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ parent gives Rwp 8.16%, triclinic WO₃ 8.39%, orthorhombic 18.04%, while tetragonal and hexagonal WO₃ scales collapse to effectively zero under bounded cells. The high-pressure P2/c CuWO₄ branch remains at ≈43.9%, so the ambient P−1 CuWO₄ model is strongly preferred. I’m mapping the remaining residual maxima against reflections from the accepted three phases before refining coordinates or specimen terms.

All remaining residual families are accounted for by accepted reflections; the largest residuals sit directly on WO₃ 22.66/23.05/23.91°, Cu₃WO₆ 15.19/19.79/32.50/33.78°, or CuWO₄ peaks. Heavy-atom refinement improves Rwp from 8.16% to 7.42% while retaining normal Cu–O and W–O coordination. I’m now testing oxygen-coordinate release phase by phase, with geometry screening and rollback if any network becomes nonphysical.

Unconstrained oxygen releases for Cu₃WO₆ and WO₃ lowered Rwp but produced impossible 1.17–1.52 Å W–O and 1.50 Å O–O contacts, so I rolled them back. Small fixed perturbations preserve octahedral geometry and give a modest gain. A common per-phase Uiso model then gives a large, physical improvement; with WO₃ capped at the stated 0.020 Å² range, Rwp is 5.82%. I’m proceeding to isotropic instrument, size, and strain terms in separate stages.

The three-phase model is now closed and physically screened. I’m evaluating the last strain/profile branches against the current 5.07% Rwp checkpoint, then I’ll freeze the best full-grid replay and export the project, CIFs, profile, and report together.

One important profile issue emerged: GSAS-II’s nominal “default” microstrain values were still contributing broadening. The Cu₃WO₆ peaks strongly require that term to collapse to zero, improving Rwp from 5.07% to 4.29%; I’m now fixing that boundary solution and re-testing the other two phases without the hidden default bias.

Residual mapping exposed a coherent family at about 35.09°, 38.32°, 48.45°, 58.11°, and 67.68°. That is the displaced tenorite CuO fingerprint, so the three-phase checkpoint was not yet phase-complete. I’m adding the exact CuO precursor now and will re-quantify all fractions before any optional line-shape terms.

With CuO included, every remaining residual maximum maps to an accepted reflection; the fit is already near counting statistics (Rwp 1.86%, expected 1.62%). The only structured mismatch is the WO₃ intensity balance—especially the 22.66/23.05/23.90° triplet—so I’m testing a single bounded March–Dollase orientation term on the plausible low-index axes, without reopening phase search.

The complete four-phase model stabilizes the light-atom refinements that previously failed: free Cu₃WO₆ and WO₃ oxygen positions now remain chemically sound (Cu–O 1.91–2.24 Å; W–O 1.71–2.16 Å) and improve Rwp more than the tiny texture correction. I’m combining those physical coordinate refinements, then I’ll re-test whether any orientation term remains warranted.

The accepted replay is now at Rwp 1.716% versus a 1.616% counting-statistics floor, with chemically valid geometries and a Cu/W inventory ratio of 1.021 (essentially the expected equimolar precursor balance). I’m freezing this model on the complete 8301-point grid and generating all deliverables directly from that same project.

The full-grid replay converged cleanly: Rwp 1.7178%, Rp 1.2808%, goodness-of-fit 1.069, with all 8301 points calculated. Final crystalline fractions are 64.918% CuWO₄, 13.150% Cu₃WO₆, 19.247% WO₃, and 2.685% CuO; I’m doing the final geometry and cross-file consistency audit now.

